traceability.evidence:
Story file docs/stories/022.0-DEV-JSDOC-COEXISTENCE.story.md defines requirements REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE, REQ-ANNOTATION-TERMINATION, REQ-JSDOC-BOUNDARY-DETECTION, REQ-CONTINUATION-LOGIC, REQ-NO-FALSE-POSITIVES, REQ-PRESERVE-MULTILINE and acceptance criteria for JSDoc coexistence, termination, boundary detection, continuation logic, error behavior, backward compatibility, docs, and tests.,Core implementation for this story lives in src/rules/valid-annotation-format.ts, function processCommentLine. It now imports isNonTraceabilityJSDocTagLine from ./helpers/valid-annotation-format-internal and contains explicit story 022 traceability:

  // Implement JSDoc tag coexistence behavior: terminate @story/@req values when a new non-traceability JSDoc tag line (e.g., @param, @returns) is encountered.
  // @supports docs/stories/022.0-DEV-JSDOC-COEXISTENCE.story.md REQ-ANNOTATION-TERMINATION REQ-CONTINUATION-LOGIC
  if (isNonTraceabilityJSDocTagLine(normalized)) {
    finalizePendingAnnotation(context, comment, options, pending);
    return null;
  }

  if (pending) {
    const continuation = normalized.trim();
    if (!continuation) {
      return pending;
    }
    const updatedValue = pending.value
      ? `${pending.value} ${continuation}`
      : continuation;
    return {
      ...pending,
      value: updatedValue,
      hasValue: pending.hasValue || continuation.length > 0,
    };
  }

This satisfies REQ-ANNOTATION-TERMINATION (terminates on boundary) and REQ-CONTINUATION-LOGIC (only continues when line does not start with a JSDoc tag).,JSDoc tag boundary detection is implemented in src/rules/helpers/valid-annotation-format-internal.ts via isNonTraceabilityJSDocTagLine:

export function isNonTraceabilityJSDocTagLine(normalized: string): boolean {
  const trimmed = normalized.trimStart();
  if (!trimmed || !trimmed.startsWith("@")) {
    return false;
  }

  if (/^@(story|req|supports)\b/.test(trimmed)) {
    return false;
  }

  return true;
}

The function treats any line whose normalized form begins with '@' and is not @story/@req/@supports as a JSDoc boundary (e.g., @param, @returns, @throws). This directly implements REQ-JSDOC-BOUNDARY-DETECTION and REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE.,Line normalization behavior is implemented in normalizeCommentLine in src/rules/helpers/valid-annotation-format-internal.ts. It trims, strips inline backtick code spans (for another story) and then either:
- Extracts from the first occurrence of @story/@req/@supports when present, or
- For non-traceability lines, strips a leading '*' and space:

  const annotationMatch = filtered.match(/@story\b|@req\b|@supports\b/);
  if (!annotationMatch || annotationMatch.index === undefined) {
    const withoutLeadingStar = filtered.replace(/^\*\s?/, "");
    return withoutLeadingStar;
  }

  return filtered.slice(annotationMatch.index);

Given the way processComment splits comment.value on newlines, this preserves line boundaries and allows isNonTraceabilityJSDocTagLine to correctly recognize JSDoc tag lines. This addresses REQ-ANNOTATION-TERMINATION and REQ-CONTINUATION-LOGIC (continuations only occur on non-tag lines).,The valid-annotation-format rule’s documentation explicitly documents JSDoc coexistence behavior and uses story 022.0 as the example. In docs/rules/valid-annotation-format.md under “JSDoc coexistence”:

- “Traceability annotations (`@story`, `@req`, `@supports`) can appear before, after, or interleaved with other JSDoc tags such as `@param`, `@returns`, and `@throws`.”
- “The parser treats any JSDoc tag line that starts with `@` (other than `@story`, `@req`, or `@supports`) as a boundary that terminates the current multi-line traceability value.”
- “Continuation lines are only considered part of the same annotation if they do not start with another JSDoc tag.”
- It provides a worked example:
  ```js
  /**
   * Calculate totals with discounting.
   * @story docs/stories/022.0-DEV-JSDOC-COEXISTENCE.story.md
   * @req REQ-CALC-TOTALS
   * @param {number[]} items
   * @param {number} discount
   * @returns {number}
   */
  function calculateTotals(items, discount) {
    /* ... */
  }
  ```

This satisfies the “Documentation” acceptance criterion and aligns with REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE / REQ-JSDOC-BOUNDARY-DETECTION / REQ-CONTINUATION-LOGIC.,Comprehensive tests for this story exist in tests/rules/valid-annotation-format.test.ts. The file header explicitly references this story and its requirements:

 * Tests for: docs/stories/022.0-DEV-JSDOC-COEXISTENCE.story.md
 * @story docs/stories/022.0-DEV-JSDOC-COEXISTENCE.story.md
 * @req REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE - Rule allows traceability annotations to coexist with other JSDoc tags
 * @req REQ-ANNOTATION-TERMINATION - Rule correctly terminates traceability annotation values at JSDoc tag boundaries
 * @req REQ-JSDOC-BOUNDARY-DETECTION - Rule detects @param/@returns and similar tags as boundaries
 * @req REQ-CONTINUATION-LOGIC - Rule correctly decides when to continue or stop multi-line traceability values
 * @req REQ-NO-FALSE-POSITIVES - Rule does not report false positives when JSDoc tags follow traceability tags
 * @req REQ-PRESERVE-MULTILINE - Rule preserves multi-line story/req values without including following JSDoc tags,Specific valid test cases in tests/rules/valid-annotation-format.test.ts cover each acceptance criterion:

- Before other tags (AC: “Before Other Tags”):
  {
    name: "[REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE] traceability before other JSDoc tags",
    code: `/**
 * @story docs/stories/022.0-DEV-JSDOC-COEXISTENCE.story.md
 * @req REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE
 * @param {string} id - Identifier for the lookup.
 * @returns {Promise} - Completes when finished.
 */
function fetchById(id) {
  return Promise.resolve();
}`,
  },

- After other tags (AC: “After Other Tags”):
  {
    name: "[REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE] traceability after other JSDoc tags",
    code: `/**
 * Fetch a user by id.
 *
 * @param {string} id - Identifier for the lookup.
 * @returns {Promise} - Completes when finished.
 * @story docs/stories/022.0-DEV-JSDOC-COEXISTENCE.story.md
 * @req REQ-ANNOTATION-TERMINATION
 */
function fetchUser(id) {
  return Promise.resolve();
}`,
  },

- Mixed positions (AC: “Mixed Positions” and REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE / REQ-JSDOC-BOUNDARY-DETECTION / REQ-CONTINUATION-LOGIC):
  {
    name: "[REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE] mixed positions of traceability and other JSDoc tags",
    code: `/**
 * Update a record with new data.
 *
 * @story docs/stories/022.0-DEV-JSDOC-COEXISTENCE.story.md
 * @param {string} id - Identifier.
 * @req REQ-JSDOC-BOUNDARY-DETECTION
 * @param {object} payload - Updated fields.
 * @returns {boolean} - True if updated.
 * @req REQ-CONTINUATION-LOGIC
 */
function updateRecord(id, payload) {
  return true;
}`,
  },

- Multi-line values with JSDoc tags after (AC: “Continuation Lines”, REQ-PRESERVE-MULTILINE):
  {
    name: "[REQ-PRESERVE-MULTILINE] multi-line @story annotation before other JSDoc tags",
    code: `/**
 * @story docs/stories/022.0-DEV-
 * JSDOC-COEXISTENCE.story.md
 * @param {string} id - Identifier for the lookup.
 * @returns {Promise} - Completes when finished.
 */
function loadForStory(id) {
  return Promise.resolve();
}`,
  },

- Regression test for the original bug (AC: “Error Messages” and REQ-NO-FALSE-POSITIVES / REQ-ANNOTATION-TERMINATION):
  {
    name: "[REQ-NO-FALSE-POSITIVES] JSDoc tags do not pollute requirement ID when following @req",
    code: `/**
 * @req REQ-OPTIMIZATION
 * @param {object} data - Input payload.
 * @returns {void}
 */
function optimize(data) {}`,
  },

This last case mirrors the Issue #3 scenario and ensures no spurious “Invalid requirement ID "REQ-OPTIMIZATION@param{object}data"” errors are produced.,The Jest test run (npm test -- --verbose) executed tests/rules/valid-annotation-format.test.ts and reports the story-022-related tests as passing:

In the test output under “Valid Annotation Format Rule (Story 005.0-DEV-ANNOTATION-VALIDATION) valid:” the following lines appear (all marked as passing):
- ✓ [REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE] traceability before other JSDoc tags
- ✓ [REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE] traceability after other JSDoc tags
- ✓ [REQ-JSDOC-TAG-COEXISTENCE] mixed positions of traceability and other JSDoc tags
- ✓ [REQ-PRESERVE-MULTILINE] multi-line @story annotation before other JSDoc tags
- ✓ [REQ-NO-FALSE-POSITIVES] JSDoc tags do not pollute requirement ID when following @req

The overall Jest summary:
- Test Suites: 1 skipped, 48 passed, 48 of 49 total
- Tests: 2 skipped, 352 passed, 354 total
confirms that all these story-022 tests are green, providing strong evidence the behavior is implemented and stable.,Backward compatibility (AC: “Backward Compatible”) is implicitly validated by the large existing suite of tests in tests/rules/valid-annotation-format.test.ts that pre-date this story (e.g., single-line and multi-line @story/@req tests, configurable pattern tests, @supports parsing tests). All of these continue to pass in the same Jest run, demonstrating that adding JSDoc boundary detection and termination behavior did not break existing valid annotations.,The rule documentation as a whole (docs/rules/valid-annotation-format.md) provides multiple examples of valid annotation usage, now including a dedicated “JSDoc coexistence” section referencing docs/stories/022.0-DEV-JSDOC-COEXISTENCE.story.md, satisfying the “Documentation” acceptance criterion for this story.,There are additional internal tests for normalization / filtering behavior in tests/rules/valid-annotation-format-internal.test.ts (for story 024.0), and nothing in those tests fails after adding the JSDoc coexistence logic, giving further confidence that multi-line and normalization behavior (REQ-PRESERVE-MULTILINE / REQ-CONTINUATION-LOGIC) remains correct and robust.
